---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'

const runtimes = [
  {
    name: 'Rust crate',
    pkg: 'etassia',
    registry: 'crates.io',
    href: 'https://crates.io/crates/etassia',
    note: 'The rendering runtime with the baked, checksummed parse artifact.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ruby gem',
    pkg: 'etassia',
    registry: 'RubyGems',
    href: 'https://rubygems.org/gems/etassia',
    note: 'The pure-Ruby reference implementation.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ruby gem',
    pkg: 'etassia-native',
    registry: 'RubyGems',
    href: 'https://rubygems.org/gems/etassia-native',
    note: 'The compiled engine as a native extension for Ruby.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Python package',
    pkg: 'etassia',
    registry: 'PyPI',
    href: 'https://pypi.org/project/etassia/',
    note: 'A ctypes binding over the C ABI of the shared library.',
  },
  {
    name: 'npm package',
    pkg: 'etassia',
    registry: 'npm',
    href: 'https://www.npmjs.com/package/etassia',
    note: 'The engine compiled to WebAssembly.',
  },
]

const repos = [
  { href: 'https://github.com/etassia/etassia', label: 'etassia/etassia', note: 'The pure-Ruby reference implementation.' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/etassia/etassia-rs', label: 'etassia/etassia-rs', note: 'The Rust runtime, the FFI and WebAssembly surfaces, and the runtime bindings.' },
  { href: 'https://github.com/etassia/etassia-mermaid-parg', label: 'etassia/etassia-mermaid-parg', note: 'The Mermaid grammar, compiled to the parse artifact the runtimes embed.' },
]
---
<Layout
  title="About — Etassia"
  description="The etymology, origin, mission and ecosystem of Etassia, a diagram-rendering engine that produces byte-identical SVG from Mermaid syntax on four runtimes."
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">
    <header class="text-center mb-12">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">About</p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Etassia draws order out of source text</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Etassia is a diagram-rendering engine for Mermaid diagram syntax. The name, the mission,
        and the engineering all point at the same idea: a diagram is order drawn out of source
        text, and the drawing should not depend on the language the drawing program is written in.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Etymology and symbolism</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        The name Etassia derives from the Ancient Greek verb
        <em>&#7940;&#954;&#964;&#940;&#963;&#963;&#969;</em> (ekt&aacute;ss&#333;), which means
        &ldquo;to draw up in formation, to array&rdquo;. The verb is compounded from
        <em>&#7940;&#954;</em> (ek), &ldquo;out of&rdquo;, and <em>&tau;&#940;&#963;&#963;&#969;</em>
        (t&aacute;ss&#333;), &ldquo;to arrange, to order&rdquo;. In classical use the verb
        describes troops drawn up in order of battle; in this project it describes the same
        operation applied to text, from which a diagram is drawn up as an ordered formation of
        nodes, edges and labels.
      </p>
      <table class="roots mt-6 w-full border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 uppercase text-xs tracking-wider">Part</th>
            <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 uppercase text-xs tracking-wider">Root</th>
            <th class="text-left py-2 px-3 bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 uppercase text-xs tracking-wider">Meaning</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 px-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>&#7940;&#954;-</code></td>
            <td class="py-2 px-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Ancient Greek <em>&#7940;&#954;</em> (ek)</td>
            <td class="py-2 px-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">out of</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 px-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><code>-&#964;&#940;&#963;&#963;&#969;</code></td>
            <td class="py-2 px-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Ancient Greek <em>&tau;&#940;&#963;&#963;&#969;</em> (t&aacute;ss&#333;)</td>
            <td class="py-2 px-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">to arrange, to order; to draw up in formation</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Origin</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Mermaid diagram syntax has become the common way to carry a diagram inside documentation:
        the source is plain text, it lives beside the prose in the repository, and it reviews like
        code. Rendering that source, however, has remained bound to one implementation, and
        documentation pipelines that are not written in JavaScript have had to shell out to an
        external process or to accept output that differs between tools.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Etassia began as a pure-Ruby implementation that renders Mermaid syntax to SVG, grown for
        the publication pipelines that Ribose maintains for standards-development organisations.
        The implementation was then ported to Rust, diagram type by diagram type, with a hand-ported
        renderer for every type in the native set. The correctness measure of the port is strict:
        on a golden corpus of 1,206 cases, the SVG the Rust runtime produces is byte-identical to
        the SVG the Ruby reference implementation produces, which removes any question of whether
        the two engines &ldquo;look the same&rdquo;.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Mission</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Etassia is designed to meet the needs of documentation pipelines that must render diagrams
        deterministically and in their own language. The engine keeps one parse artifact, compiled
        ahead of time and checksummed, so that parsing does not drift between deployments. It keeps
        one native boundary, a C ABI of three functions, so that adding a runtime binding is a thin
        act of binding rather than a reimplementation. And it measures every renderer against the
        reference implementation on the golden corpus, so that identical input yields identical
        SVG on Ruby, Rust, Python and WebAssembly.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        The boundary of this mission is stated explicitly. Types in the Mermaid syntax for which
        the Rust runtime has no renderer yet are detected and reported through the Unported error
        class, which the bindings translate into a fallback call to the reference Ruby
        implementation; the syntax page names the types that are in this state in the current
        release.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4">The ecosystem</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Every project lives at
        <a class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 underline underline-offset-2" href="https://github.com/etassia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/etassia</a>.
        The packages are published under the plain name <code>etassia</code> in each registry,
        except the Ruby compiled-engine gem, which is named <code>etassia-native</code>.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
        <div class="ecosystem-block rounded-xl bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Runtime packages</h3>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm">
            {runtimes.map((r) => (
              <li class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                <a class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium" href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{r.pkg}</a>
                <span class="text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-500"> ({r.registry})</span>
                <span class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400"> &mdash; {r.note}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ecosystem-block rounded-xl bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white">Repositories</h3>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm">
            {repos.map((r) => (
              <li class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
                <a class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 underline underline-offset-2 font-medium" href={r.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{r.label}</a>
                <span class="text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400"> &mdash; {r.note}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Open source</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Etassia is BSD-2-Clause licensed and maintained by Ribose and the broader community. The
        golden corpus that carries the byte-identical parity property is part of the runtime
        repository, so a contribution to a renderer can be checked against the same measure the
        maintainers use. Contributions are welcome through the repositories of the organization.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Get started</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        Install the runtime for your language and render a first diagram, or read a diagram being
        rendered live in the playground.
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 text-sm p-4 overflow-x-auto"><code>cargo add etassia
gem install etassia
pip install etassia
npm install etassia</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-4 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300">
        <a class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 underline underline-offset-2" href="/playground">Open the playground</a>
        to edit Mermaid source and watch the engine render it in the browser, or
        <a class="text-primary-600 dark:text-primary-400 underline underline-offset-2" href="/syntax">read the syntax page</a>
        for the full list of supported diagram types.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .roots th {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
